# SpecFold — Angular

Follow **existing** project layout (modules vs standalone, state library). Do not introduce a second architectural style in one feature without an explicit spec note.

## Structure & modules

- Prefer **standalone components** and explicit `imports[]` when the repo already does; otherwise match **NgModule** patterns in-tree.
- **Lazy-load** feature routes; keep the root shell light.
- **Smart vs dumb:** smart containers load data and handle side effects; presentational components use `@Input` / `@Output` only.

## State & async

- Match the repo: **Signals**, **RxJS-only**, or **NgRx** — pick one strategy per feature.
- Prefer **`AsyncPipe`** in templates to avoid subscription leaks.
- RxJS: `switchMap` when later emissions should cancel prior work; `exhaustMap` for double-submit guards; `catchError` at HTTP boundaries.

## Forms & a11y

- **Reactive forms** for multi-field or async validation; align client validators with server rules when the spec says so.
- Errors: associate messages with controls (`aria-describedby`, invalid state on inputs).

## Security

- **Never** render untrusted HTML without sanitization; use `DomSanitizer` only for known-safe patterns.
- Avoid tokens in URLs and logs; use HTTP-only cookies or headers per project auth design.

## Performance

- Use **`ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush`** on presentational components when inputs are immutable or observables drive the view.
- Avoid heavy sync work in constructors; use `ngOnInit` / `effect()` / `afterNextRender` as appropriate.
- Respect **bundle budgets** in `angular.json`.

## Tests

- Unit: **Jest** or **Karma/Jasmine** per project; assert **user-visible behavior** tied to spec acceptance, not private fields.
- E2E: **Cypress** or **Playwright** only where the spec calls for critical flows.
